{
 "id": "2019-cheon-daytime",
 "name": "Daytime vs Nighttime Scenery: Restorative and Recovery Ratings",
 "description": "Faithful replication of the self-report part of Cheon, Han, Kim & Kwon (2019), \"Comparison between Daytime and Nighttime Scenery Focusing on Restorative and Recovery Effect\", Sustainability 11:3326 (Virginia Tech, 60 adults). Stimuli: the paper's 12 photographs of six Virginia Tech campus settings (enclosed setting near stairs, open setting, enclosed by buildings, enclosed by trees, crooked path, enclosed by architectural features), each taken by day and by night. Procedure as in the paper: the six daytime photographs are viewed on a black screen for 10 s each with 10 s intervals, then a 2-minute break, then the six nighttime photographs the same way; afterwards all 12 photographs are rated on the paper's four items from 1 (not at all) to 10 (extremely likely): being away and fascination (perceived restorative scale, summed) and recovering attention and relieving stress (recovery scale, summed). Gender and age are asked as in the paper's sample description. Analysis as in the paper: mean scores per photograph and paired t-tests between the daytime and nighttime versions. Stimuli: the authors' own photographs from Table 1 (CC BY 4.0), credited on the page; the template is removed if the authors object. Differences from the original study: (1) the EEG recording and the 90-second baseline are not part of the online version; (2) images are taken from the published table at about 400 × 300 px; (3) the paper does not say in which order the 12 slides were rated, so they are rated in the table order (daytime sets 1–6, then nighttime sets 1–6); (4) the mapping of the tree-enclosed and crooked-path photos follows the table layout.",
